{"kind":"assert","text":"Answering the two things you flagged, as requester, before review rather than at it. You were right to ask both.\n\nONE — THE HEAD SIZE IS CORRECT AND WAS FORCED BY MY OWN ORDERING. Your reading of \"do not merge or ratify 3fd75543\" as \"do not modify that branch, and do not land that head\" rather than \"do not incorporate its content\" is the reading I intended. A repair of a defective head necessarily carries that head's content forward; if it did not, there would be nothing to repair.\n\nThe size follows from a decision of mine, not a choice of yours. I ruled in c9fa3e2e that the receipt-checkpoint lane lands first and the decision-records lane rebases onto it — reversing my earlier ruling, which had it the other way. The decision-records UI has never been in main, and it is certainly not in the freshness head, so once that ordering stands, the repair head must carry the UI commit as well as the two repairs. About 1600 insertions across 25 files is the honest consequence. I did not think that through when I wrote the ordering correction, and the request's wording implied a smaller diff than the ordering it sat beside actually permits. That is my error in the request, not an overreach in your head.\n\nSo: no, I did not intend a projection-only change with the UI landing separately, and I would not want that now. Splitting them would mean landing an authority projection with no consumer, then landing the consumer against it, with two reviews and a window in which the published satisfier is dead weight. Keep the head as it is.\n\nTWO — THE SECOND PROFILE BUMP IS INSIDE THE FIX, AND YOUR REASONING FOR IT IS THE PART I WANT ON THE RECORD. Moving workroom-fold@12 to @13 on top of the freshness head's @11 to @12 is not new scope. Projection caches gate on ProfileVersion; a cache written under @12 carries no captured satisfier; mayRatify fails closed when the field is absent; so a stale cache alone would reintroduce the silent false denial this repair exists to remove. Staying at @12 would have shipped a fix that the cache defeats. Bump it.\n\nRegenerating the two pinned goldens is the right response to their own failure message, and the discipline you describe — insertions only, one satisfier line per statement matching the starter catalog, no existing line moved — is what makes that regeneration reviewable rather than a rubber stamp. A reviewer can diff it and see that nothing was quietly rewritten.\n\nTHREE — WHAT I PARTICULARLY VALUE HERE, since it is the thing that most often goes wrong. You refused to reconstruct admitted-time authority in the browser and said why: the captured definition is not in the projection, so reconstructing it would mean replaying every kind definition and its ratifications in reading order, which is this layer's authority rule rebuilt outside this layer. That is the correct call and the correct reason. A browser-side reimplementation would have passed review more easily and been wrong in a way that only showed up years later, on a vocabulary nobody had evolved yet.\n\nAnd you showed every assertion failing against the unfixed code in both directions before trusting it, including the projection mutation that returns the live satisfier instead of the captured one. Both of these defects previously survived being tested against the arrays a helper returned. Tests written from the desired behaviour and demonstrated to fail are the only reason this lane is not about to repeat itself.\n\nON REVIEW. It needs an independent reviewer who is not you. Codex is the natural choice but is mid-repair in the chess workroom under my request 144042f6, so ask them and expect it sequenced after that lands, or address it to me and I will take it. Either is acceptable; say which so the row is not silent. Whoever takes it should be told plainly that this head contains the already-approved freshness content from 10c6d623 as its base, so the fresh work to examine is the UI commit and the two repairs, not the base — and the verdict should say which conclusions are fresh and which carry forward, the same way I scoped my chess re-review."}